## Proof

**Construction.** For each positive integer $a$, write $a = 2^{v_2(a)} \cdot m$ with $m$ odd. Define $x_a \in \mathbb{Z}/a\mathbb{Z}$ to be the unique residue class modulo $a$ (guaranteed by the Chinese Remainder Theorem) satisfying
$$x_a \equiv 1 \pmod{2^{v_2(a)}}, \qquad x_a \equiv 0 \pmod{m}.$$

**Compatibility.** Suppose $a \mid b$. Write $b = 2^{v_2(b)} \cdot n$ with $n$ odd; then $v_2(a) \le v_2(b)$ and $m \mid n$. Since $x_b \equiv 1 \pmod{2^{v_2(b)}}$, we have $x_b \equiv 1 \pmod{2^{v_2(a)}}$; and since $x_b \equiv 0 \pmod{n}$, we have $x_b \equiv 0 \pmod{m}$. By uniqueness in CRT, $x_b \equiv x_a \pmod{a}$, i.e. $f_{ab}(x_b) = x_a$. Hence $(x_a)_{a \ge 1}$ is a compatible family and defines an element $\alpha \in \widehat{\mathbb{Z}}$.

**$\alpha$ is not of the form $z_c$.** Suppose for contradiction that $\alpha = z_c$ for some $c \in \mathbb{Z}$, so $c \equiv x_a \pmod{a}$ for every $a \ge 1$.

For every odd $m \ge 1$ (taking $a = m$, so $v_2(a)=0$ and $m$ is the odd part), we get $c \equiv 0 \pmod{m}$. In particular $c \equiv 0 \pmod{p}$ for every odd prime $p$. An integer divisible by infinitely many distinct primes must be $c = 0$.

But taking $a = 2$: $x_2 \equiv 1 \pmod{2}$, so $c \equiv 1 \pmod{2}$, i.e. $c$ is odd. This contradicts $c = 0$.

Therefore $\alpha = (x_a)_{a \ge 1}$ is an explicit member of $\widehat{\mathbb{Z}}$ that is not of the form $z_c$ for any $c \in \mathbb{Z}$. $\blacksquare$

